WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.999 --> 00:00:09.240
If a ship was to capsize,

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:12.090
they don't just turn turtle quickly.

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:13.620
They fill up with water.

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:16.830
And for the vessel to turn
that quickly so that people

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:19.470
would have to hang on to
things, not believable.

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:21.420
Hi, I'm Capt. Wendy Williams.

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:24.270
Recently, I was the first Canadian woman

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:27.030
to captain a mega cruise ship.

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:29.490
Today, we'll be looking
at cruise ship disasters

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:34.062
in movies and TV and deciding
how realistic they are.

00:00:34.062 --> 00:00:35.562
It's going to hit!

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:41.865
Jack: Get back!

00:00:41.865 --> 00:00:44.370
The Titanic disaster is the one

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:46.980
that everybody associates
with marine disasters.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:48.540
First of all, it struck an iceberg.

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:50.370
I'm hoping it would never happen again,

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:53.070
but I don't, I never say
it will never happen again.

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:55.200
We just have a lot more technology

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:58.170
and we have associations

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:02.610
and the ability to track some
of the bigger icebergs now.

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:05.820
I don't think that the crew of the Titanic

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:07.530
were even expecting ice.

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:10.138
Whereas now, it's part of the training.

00:01:10.138 --> 00:01:11.779
They're closing the doors!

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:16.980
With any incidents on board,

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:18.270
there's checklists of things,

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:19.793
but there are initial actions

00:01:19.793 --> 00:01:23.850
for something that may
involve striking or grounding

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:25.680
or hitting another vessel.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.560
And that is to close all
the watertight doors.

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:30.840
What they called watertight compartments

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:32.610
were not actually watertight,

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:35.280
so they actually put doors through

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:37.380
what should've been
watertight compartments.

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:40.473
And what happened was that the
vessel progressively flooded.

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:49.290
If a ship were to have a strike,

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:53.371
there may be the need to be

00:01:53.371 --> 00:01:56.855
in a water-filled or water-filling area

00:01:56.855 --> 00:01:59.790
and have to do exactly
what Jack and Rose did.

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:01.950
Unfortunately, they got
to the top of the stairs

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:04.375
and things were locked.
That doesn't happen.

00:02:04.375 --> 00:02:08.280
There are emergency escapes
that everybody is trained for.

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:10.255
You have to hold an emergency drill.

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:15.278
Jack: I've got you. I won't let go.

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:20.122
Is it advisable to go up or down?

00:02:20.122 --> 00:02:21.360
I mean, the ship is sinking.

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:22.650
You're going to do what
you're going to do.

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:25.385
The fight-or-flight kicks in,

00:02:25.385 --> 00:02:29.430
and we would be just grappling
to find the highest point

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:32.730
before succumbing to
having to be in the water.

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:35.195
Some people actually

00:02:35.195 --> 00:02:37.440
in a situation where the
ship is not actually sinking,

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:39.660
they choose that jumping into the water

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:42.060
is the safest method.

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:44.400
My view and what I tell everybody

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:46.110
and when we're going through training is

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:48.360
your ship is your biggest lifeboat.

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:50.130
You don't want to be in
one of those little boats

00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:52.110
unless you absolutely have to.

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:53.876
In this case, a lot of people died

00:02:53.876 --> 00:02:58.200
because they could not
safely get off the vessel.

00:02:58.200 --> 00:02:59.640
I think it's an eight.

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:02.998
It was really well done,
and I would imagine

00:03:02.998 --> 00:03:06.488
that it was very similar to that event.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:12.630
I've seen the movie. That's food illness.

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:14.370
I've never been on a ship

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:15.990
where there's been an issue with food.

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:19.170
The food and the food preparation,

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:21.000
it's very highly regulated.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:24.570
Everybody has to have special
food-preparation courses.

00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:27.912
I'm sure it does happen, it
just isn't cause for alarm.

00:03:27.912 --> 00:03:30.300
Cruise ships have been in the news a lot

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:32.763
for something called norovirus.

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:36.900
Typically, the cruise ship
does not have norovirus on it.

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:39.093
People bring norovirus to the ship.

00:03:44.132 --> 00:03:46.225
There's a guy bringing out an AED

00:03:46.225 --> 00:03:47.820
that just happens to have one.

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:50.160
Crew members would, even
though they're trained,

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:52.020
they would just be making
people comfortable.

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:55.620
They wouldn't actually be
doing solo medical procedures.

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:57.150
On a vessel of that size,

00:03:57.150 --> 00:03:59.370
there would be a dedicated medical crew.

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:01.380
Typically, they would
carry a doctor and a nurse

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:03.785
and have some sort of triage place.

00:04:03.785 --> 00:04:05.486
Hand grenade.

00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:13.053
Piracy is a thing.
There are pirate attacks

00:04:13.053 --> 00:04:15.420
of many different types
of vessels in the world.

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:18.330
Cruise ships are not exempt from that.

00:04:18.330 --> 00:04:19.950
I mean, there's all
different shapes and sizes

00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:22.140
of how vessels do navigate through areas

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:24.060
where there is a risk of piracy.

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:25.200
There are agencies

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:28.620
that actually put people
on board with weapons.

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:32.927
There's barbed wire on
deck, charged fire hoses.

00:04:32.927 --> 00:04:34.740
It can get pretty nasty.

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:36.390
The pirates themselves,

00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:38.820
some of them are equipped with
things like rocket launchers

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:42.033
and really bad weaponry
that can do a lot of damage.

00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:46.020
I'm assuming the explosion at
the stern of the vessel here

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:48.120
happened because of one grenade.

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:50.460
I don't think a grenade
would be able to take down

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:52.350
a vessel of that size, one of them.

00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:53.913
Believability here,

00:04:55.050 --> 00:04:56.550
probably four, five,

00:04:56.550 --> 00:04:59.130
for how the situations were being handled

00:04:59.130 --> 00:05:01.547
and the actual attack itself.

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:06.090
Laser imaging generating

00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:08.040
a negative white-screen response, sir.

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:08.944
That's impossible.

00:05:08.944 --> 00:05:12.210
Radios, there are so many backups,

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:14.370
and if the radios didn't work,
you have satellite phones,

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:17.040
all sorts of different types
of satellite communication.

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:20.943
To be able to scramble
a ship like that, no.

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:23.822
What's happening here, gentlemen?

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:26.940
There's a lot of people on the bridge.

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:28.650
I'm not sure what they're all doing.

00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:30.933
Most cruise companies,
not saying all of them,

00:05:30.933 --> 00:05:32.220
they have different terminology,

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:33.780
but we would have what's called

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:36.330
green, yellow, and red conditions.

00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:40.290
Green would be when
everything is smooth sailing.

00:05:40.290 --> 00:05:44.130
Typically you would have a
navigation officer up there,

00:05:44.130 --> 00:05:46.980
you would have an assistant
or a co-navigator,

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:48.720
and you would have one or two lookouts.

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:50.310
That would be the bridge composition.

00:05:50.310 --> 00:05:54.990
Our bridges, like, a full red condition,

00:05:54.990 --> 00:05:58.482
would be captain, staff captain,

00:05:58.482 --> 00:06:01.410
chief mate or communicator, two lookouts.

00:06:01.410 --> 00:06:02.428
That's it. Five.

00:06:02.428 --> 00:06:04.762
Five of you on the bridge,
handling the situation.

00:06:04.762 --> 00:06:06.330
Probably just a pod of whales.

00:06:06.330 --> 00:06:09.272
Moving at 31 knots, sir? I don't think so.

00:06:09.272 --> 00:06:10.380
Where's it coming from?

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:11.480
Directly beneath us.

00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:16.650
This particular cruise ship
is equipped with sonar,

00:06:16.650 --> 00:06:18.566
which, I don't know

00:06:18.566 --> 00:06:20.282
why you would even need
that. You wouldn't.

00:06:20.282 --> 00:06:22.525
So, everything is scrambled

00:06:22.525 --> 00:06:25.470
except for the sonar
that's picking up some,

00:06:25.470 --> 00:06:27.360
I guess it's a sea monster in this one.

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:30.150
Even though in this particular movie clip,

00:06:30.150 --> 00:06:33.030
they're dealing with a sea monster,

00:06:33.030 --> 00:06:36.795
not to put whales or large
mammals into a category

00:06:36.795 --> 00:06:38.100
of being sea monsters,

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:40.620
very unfortunately,
there are whale strikes.

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:44.100
Cruise ships pay a lot of attention to

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:46.108
give a large leave distance

00:06:46.108 --> 00:06:48.333
and to avoid whales at all costs.

00:06:48.333 --> 00:06:52.533
Believability from that
bridge scene, probably zero.

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:01.830
This one looked like
Niagara Falls on its side.

00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:03.360
Not a believable wave.

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:06.960
A tsunami is biggest when
it kind of hits the shore.

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:09.560
Out at sea, a tsunami might just be

00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:10.980
a wave like any other wave.

00:07:10.980 --> 00:07:14.310
So the fact that this is a
tsunami and not a rogue wave,

00:07:14.310 --> 00:07:16.200
yeah, probably not that believable.

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:18.198
Being that a tsunami wave
wouldn't look like that,

00:07:18.198 --> 00:07:22.830
you wouldn't necessarily navigate
away from the actual wave,

00:07:22.830 --> 00:07:25.470
but you would navigate away

00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:28.261
from the area of concern if you could.

00:07:37.140 --> 00:07:38.670
I do remember seeing this as a kid,

00:07:38.670 --> 00:07:40.530
and I remember the dining-room scene

00:07:40.530 --> 00:07:43.200
and everything going upside down.

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:46.290
Are tables and chairs and
pianos and things secured?

00:07:46.290 --> 00:07:48.090
Cruise ships, larger vessels,

00:07:48.090 --> 00:07:50.850
they have something called a heavy weather

00:07:50.850 --> 00:07:52.890
or a heavy object register list.

00:07:52.890 --> 00:07:56.010
And yes, larger, big-ticket items

00:07:56.010 --> 00:07:57.870
that can cause a lot of
damage, like a piano,

00:07:57.870 --> 00:08:00.960
could literally go through
a wall, a bulkhead.

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:02.969
So they are secured.

00:08:02.969 --> 00:08:06.486
Tables and chairs, no,
they would be moveable.

00:08:06.486 --> 00:08:09.060
And for the vessel to turn that quickly

00:08:09.060 --> 00:08:11.340
so that people would
have to hang onto things;

00:08:11.340 --> 00:08:12.360
not believable.

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:14.760
If a ship was to capsize,

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:17.610
they don't just turn turtle quickly.

00:08:17.610 --> 00:08:19.680
They fill up with water.

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:21.690
All right, now what?

00:08:21.690 --> 00:08:25.680
We're in shaft alley. Nowhere
is the steel hull thinner.

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:27.900
This hull is only 1 inch thick.

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:30.360
Would there be a sustained amount of time

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:32.970
that people could have
survived inside there?

00:08:32.970 --> 00:08:36.588
I mean, possibly yes.
There would be air pockets.

00:08:36.588 --> 00:08:39.120
The likelihood of there
being that much air,

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:41.552
that much time, highly unlikely.

00:08:41.552 --> 00:08:43.410
The shaft alley, that's interesting,

00:08:43.410 --> 00:08:47.220
because the shell-plating
thickness is "only 1 inch."

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:50.496
That is probably icebreaker class.

00:08:50.496 --> 00:08:53.790
One inch would be exceptionally thick.

00:08:53.790 --> 00:08:56.790
Ship hulls are not that thick typically,

00:08:56.790 --> 00:09:00.050
but in engine spaces, technical
spaces, they would be.

00:09:00.050 --> 00:09:03.390
Thickness varies depending
on how the ship is built

00:09:03.390 --> 00:09:04.770
and for what class.

00:09:04.770 --> 00:09:07.590
I'd probably give it a nine
as far as fun movies go;

00:09:07.590 --> 00:09:10.230
believability, two, three.

00:09:10.230 --> 00:09:13.530
The deadly Pandora virus
is spreading rapidly.

00:09:13.530 --> 00:09:17.520
Unprecedented threat requires
a worldwide quarantine.

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:18.372
A virus?!

00:09:18.372 --> 00:09:19.505
Stop the ship!

00:09:19.505 --> 00:09:22.695
Given that it was created in 2012 --

00:09:22.695 --> 00:09:25.050
the pandemic took everyone by surprise,

00:09:25.050 --> 00:09:27.270
and certainly cruise ships

00:09:27.270 --> 00:09:28.800
that were already in mid-cruise

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:32.610
suddenly were getting cases
of the virus on board.

00:09:32.610 --> 00:09:35.209
The Diamond Princess was very unfortunate

00:09:35.209 --> 00:09:37.410
that she had a lot of cases on board

00:09:37.410 --> 00:09:39.450
and then was put under quarantine.

00:09:39.450 --> 00:09:41.520
She was kind of the worst-case scenario.

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:43.950
Ships are now being
constructed with ventilation

00:09:43.950 --> 00:09:46.110
such that it's independent in cabins,

00:09:46.110 --> 00:09:49.383
filtration systems, much like
the airline industry did.

00:09:50.670 --> 00:09:54.270
Announcer: As we greet a
sunny day 12 after virus!

00:09:54.270 --> 00:09:56.010
Today's delicious buffet

00:09:56.010 --> 00:09:59.850
features fresh seagull and
hull-scraped barnacles.

00:09:59.850 --> 00:10:02.843
Would the ship look like
that after 12 days? No.

00:10:02.843 --> 00:10:05.580
But in "The Simpsons," where
all that's absolutely perfect.

00:10:05.580 --> 00:10:08.270
Vessels could only be
out at sea for as long

00:10:08.270 --> 00:10:11.220
as their fuel and food supply
would allow them to be.

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:15.510
We didn't and we wouldn't
keep people past that point.

00:10:15.510 --> 00:10:18.510
Cruise ships do carry a lot of food,

00:10:18.510 --> 00:10:21.120
and they carry food in excess

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:23.550
of what the voyage length would be,

00:10:23.550 --> 00:10:24.780
but not by that much.

00:10:24.780 --> 00:10:26.130
So I'd probably give that a nine,

00:10:26.130 --> 00:10:28.410
just because it's actually pretty spot-on

00:10:28.410 --> 00:10:32.024
for having been made almost
a decade before our pandemic.

00:10:32.024 --> 00:10:33.770
No, don't let him drink that!

00:10:33.770 --> 00:10:36.536
Don't let him drink our torpedo juice.

00:10:38.970 --> 00:10:41.790
They're in a ship's jail, or a brig.

00:10:41.790 --> 00:10:43.470
Ours look a lot different than that.

00:10:43.470 --> 00:10:45.570
You wouldn't be thrown
in there with others.

00:10:45.570 --> 00:10:46.680
There are holding cells,

00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:50.160
and they are typically a
cabin without furniture,

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:51.840
with a mattress on the ground,

00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:54.119
and they're under camera surveillance

00:10:54.119 --> 00:10:57.661
and they have a window, a
non-break window, in a door.

00:10:57.661 --> 00:10:59.670
Typically, they're not
in there for very long.

00:10:59.670 --> 00:11:01.830
It could be just the night
before we get to port.

00:11:01.830 --> 00:11:03.690
But there's a lot of factors

00:11:03.690 --> 00:11:07.410
before we could actually put
somebody in a kind of time-out.

00:11:07.410 --> 00:11:11.650
Whatever the situation
is, it's to mitigate

00:11:11.650 --> 00:11:14.160
anybody else getting
hurt from their actions

00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:15.780
or from them harming themselves.

00:11:15.780 --> 00:11:16.800
This is a big cruise ship!

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:18.347
It's got, like, a big, deep sound.

00:11:18.347 --> 00:11:20.640
It's like a [imitating engine starting].

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:21.960
Yeah, if you're starting the engine.

00:11:21.960 --> 00:11:25.833
But we would be hearing a steady
"guh, guh, guh, guh, guh."

00:11:25.833 --> 00:11:29.233
The guy that was doing the kind of low

00:11:29.233 --> 00:11:31.225
"boom, boom, boom" is probably closest.

00:11:31.225 --> 00:11:33.150
It depends what kind of
propulsion they have.

00:11:33.150 --> 00:11:37.170
So if they have big
diesel engines working,

00:11:37.170 --> 00:11:39.420
you may have that guy that was generating

00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:41.040
that "boom, boom, boom, boom" sound.

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:43.980
It's almost a comforting
sound. I find it comforting.

00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:47.070
[alarm going off]

00:11:47.070 --> 00:11:48.453
You wouldn't be left alone,

00:11:49.710 --> 00:11:51.780
so that was a little unrealistic.

00:11:51.780 --> 00:11:53.086
Kind of a common thread here,

00:11:53.086 --> 00:11:57.270
everything on a cruise ship
is done with checklists

00:11:57.270 --> 00:11:59.160
so that we don't forget people,

00:11:59.160 --> 00:12:01.800
we don't forget to sweep areas.

00:12:01.800 --> 00:12:04.050
It would be part of the
security team's protocol

00:12:04.050 --> 00:12:06.300
to escort them to a muster area.

00:12:06.300 --> 00:12:07.350
That would not happen.

00:12:07.350 --> 00:12:11.283
Believability of the jail
scene, not believable.

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:17.400
This is dealing with the rogue wave.

00:12:17.400 --> 00:12:19.410
For a long time, people
didn't even believe

00:12:19.410 --> 00:12:21.330
that rogue waves actually existed.

00:12:21.330 --> 00:12:22.950
It's an anomaly wave

00:12:22.950 --> 00:12:26.130
that is different from
all of the other waves

00:12:26.130 --> 00:12:27.600
in a certain sea state.

00:12:27.600 --> 00:12:31.886
And then it could overturn
vessels or come at vessels.

00:12:31.886 --> 00:12:33.331
I got it! I got it!

00:12:33.331 --> 00:12:34.963
Come on.

00:12:34.963 --> 00:12:35.929
Turn.

00:12:39.606 --> 00:12:43.680
Basically, the tip, or the bow,

00:12:43.680 --> 00:12:45.870
has what's called a collision bulkhead,

00:12:45.870 --> 00:12:48.210
and it's very strong construction.

00:12:48.210 --> 00:12:50.970
You never really want to
take anything broadside,

00:12:50.970 --> 00:12:53.430
because even in a small sea,

00:12:53.430 --> 00:12:57.300
a vessel can get into
what's called synchronous,

00:12:57.300 --> 00:12:58.350
a synchronicity,

00:12:58.350 --> 00:13:00.650
which will cause it to
become very unstable.

00:13:05.586 --> 00:13:07.350
The announcements were not appropriate.

00:13:07.350 --> 00:13:09.960
I felt that they caused
panic. And as a captain,

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:11.970
you want everybody to
know what's happening,

00:13:11.970 --> 00:13:16.875
but there are ways to word
things to make people feel

00:13:16.875 --> 00:13:19.500
like their understanding of the situation,

00:13:19.500 --> 00:13:21.810
this is what's happening,

00:13:21.810 --> 00:13:25.140
but I am in the hands
of a very trained crew,

00:13:25.140 --> 00:13:27.781
and if I follow
instruction, I will be safe.

00:13:27.781 --> 00:13:29.940
You would have crew members

00:13:29.940 --> 00:13:32.853
whose duty it would be
to do crowd control.

00:13:37.950 --> 00:13:39.783
Cruise ships have a fantastic

00:13:39.783 --> 00:13:42.240
stability criteria, they call it,

00:13:42.240 --> 00:13:45.873
and it would take a h--- of a
lot to capsize one of these.

00:13:45.873 --> 00:13:49.548
Is it going to happen as a result of the

00:13:49.548 --> 00:13:53.153
general rogue waves that
I've read about? Not really.

00:13:53.153 --> 00:13:56.160
Now, if a cruise ship
were to go off vertical,

00:13:56.160 --> 00:13:59.506
these areas that are not
watertight would fill with water,

00:13:59.506 --> 00:14:01.110
causing the capsize.

00:14:01.110 --> 00:14:03.930
A good example of that we may have seen

00:14:03.930 --> 00:14:05.490
that maybe people will understand

00:14:05.490 --> 00:14:07.140
is the Costa Concordia.

00:14:07.140 --> 00:14:10.890
She went over, and she
would've kept going over

00:14:10.890 --> 00:14:13.833
were it not for the depth
of water that she was in.

00:14:18.810 --> 00:14:20.970
That's a little bit of a movie thing.

00:14:20.970 --> 00:14:22.920
You're not going to really get explosions,

00:14:22.920 --> 00:14:24.718
because explosions would have to do

00:14:24.718 --> 00:14:29.676
with vapors catching
fire somehow or igniting

00:14:29.676 --> 00:14:31.770
and a large amount of vapors

00:14:31.770 --> 00:14:34.290
in an area causing
these random explosions.

00:14:34.290 --> 00:14:35.550
And on cruise ships,

00:14:35.550 --> 00:14:37.770
you just don't have that
in the accommodation.

00:14:37.770 --> 00:14:41.100
I can't think of something that
would cause those fireballs.

00:14:41.100 --> 00:14:43.020
Although exciting, very exciting.

00:14:43.020 --> 00:14:45.510
Believability of what was going on,

00:14:45.510 --> 00:14:48.960
probably a five, four,

00:14:48.960 --> 00:14:50.220
somewhere in that realm.

00:14:50.220 --> 00:14:52.023
But fun. Fun to watch.

00:14:54.260 --> 00:14:56.178
Ready.

00:14:57.720 --> 00:14:58.800
Now.

00:14:58.800 --> 00:15:01.500
For somebody to be able to control

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:04.470
the main engines of a vessel, no,

00:15:04.470 --> 00:15:07.800
they're not hooked in
to an automation system

00:15:07.800 --> 00:15:09.540
that anybody would be able to take over.

00:15:09.540 --> 00:15:12.240
There's automated systems on board,

00:15:12.240 --> 00:15:13.860
but everything can be done manually.

00:15:13.860 --> 00:15:14.693
What are these switches here?

00:15:14.693 --> 00:15:15.570
Don't you touch them!

00:15:15.570 --> 00:15:18.253
They open the ballast doors.
You want to flood the ship?

00:15:20.190 --> 00:15:21.450
There aren't ballast doors.

00:15:21.450 --> 00:15:24.150
There's watertight doors
and there's ballast tanks.

00:15:24.150 --> 00:15:26.901
Those are the tanks that
you can fill with water

00:15:26.901 --> 00:15:30.780
to change the trim of a vessel.

00:15:30.780 --> 00:15:32.340
Someone's just taken the wrong term

00:15:32.340 --> 00:15:33.998
and put it with the wrong equipment.

00:15:39.870 --> 00:15:42.510
It'd have to have been going
at a very, very fast rate

00:15:42.510 --> 00:15:44.280
for it to sort of glance off like that,

00:15:44.280 --> 00:15:47.145
but the amount of speed,

00:15:47.145 --> 00:15:50.160
and the two vessels would've
been fairly crumpled.

00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:52.200
There was a few black
marks along the side,

00:15:52.200 --> 00:15:54.363
not even a crumpled bow.

00:15:54.363 --> 00:15:55.830
Yeah, not really believable.

00:15:55.830 --> 00:15:58.740
For the cargo vessel to have
an anchor just swinging loose,

00:15:58.740 --> 00:16:00.450
that wouldn't happen.

00:16:00.450 --> 00:16:02.850
Anchors are either stowed
or they're deployed.

00:16:02.850 --> 00:16:05.040
The cargo vessel was not moving,

00:16:05.040 --> 00:16:07.770
and there was no sea to
make the anchor swing.

00:16:07.770 --> 00:16:10.470
Those things weigh tons and tons.

00:16:10.470 --> 00:16:15.030
Believability on a scale
to 10, maybe three, two.

00:16:15.030 --> 00:16:19.230
Not a lot there, meat on the
bones, for an actual incident.

00:16:19.230 --> 00:16:21.750
My favorite cruise ship scene from today

00:16:21.750 --> 00:16:23.310
would have to be "Titanic."

00:16:23.310 --> 00:16:25.160
It's the basis for

00:16:25.160 --> 00:16:29.493
all the safety protocol that we do today,

00:16:29.493 --> 00:16:32.550
and I think the portrayal
of what was happening,

00:16:32.550 --> 00:16:34.145
the severity of the situation,

00:16:34.145 --> 00:16:36.090
was very readily understood.

00:16:36.090 --> 00:16:38.580
If you have enjoyed this today,

00:16:38.580 --> 00:16:40.590
all you have to do is click the link above

00:16:40.590 --> 00:16:43.007
and set sail for another one.

